Tested in scratch repos with a minimal global config and
GIT_EDITOR=true: two fixup commits for two commits of a merged side branch, one of them
sharing its subject with a commit on the first-parent line. The check
reported the tree identical and "result ok", the merge was redone, and
each fix landed in its target. Notes on the folded commits survived.

The advice for a fix that conflicts twice was tested too: commit T
writes a1, a later commit L adds a2 after it, and the fix changes a1 to
a1fix. The fold conflicts with the incoming side carrying a2; resolving
it to a1fix alone, then resolving L to a1fix plus a2, gives a tree
identical to the old tip, "result ok", and T holding a1fix.

The old fold route, fixup=<sha>:<target>, still refuses to cross a
merge after this commit; the next commit removes it.
